# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRUST ANCHORS. Not ordinary files.
#
# `pubkey` in tauri.conf.json is the minisign key the in-app updater checks
# every package against, and `endpoints` is where it looks. update.rs binds
# every offer to its release name and version. Change any of these and an
# installed app will accept a package the release key did not sign, or look
# for updates on a host we do not control. No ordinary pull request edits them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/app/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json              @MendeMatthias
/app/src-tauri/src/update.rs                @MendeMatthias
# What the web view may call. One more permission is one more thing a model's
# output could reach.
/app/src-tauri/capabilities/                @MendeMatthias

# The engine pins. These decide which btx-modeld and which llama.cpp every user
# runs, by sha256. A changed pin says where the new hash was measured.
/app/scripts/stage-engine.sh                @MendeMatthias
/app/scripts/stage-engine-linux.sh          @MendeMatthias
/app/scripts/stage-engine-windows.sh        @MendeMatthias
/app/scripts/build-engine-macos.sh          @MendeMatthias

# Which host every AInode is given, which models it offers, and where it
# checks in. A change here changes who sees every user's address.
/app/src-tauri/resources/hosts.json         @MendeMatthias
/app/src-tauri/resources/catalog.json       @MendeMatthias
/app/src-tauri/src/backend/keeper.rs        @MendeMatthias

# CI must NEVER hold a signing key, and it does not: this repository has no
# Actions secrets and no environments, and no workflow references any.
# Releases are signed on the maintainer's machine and that is the design.
# What this rule guards against is a change that adds `secrets.`,
# `permissions: write`, or a `pull_request_target` trigger.
